"executive_summary": "This study investigates the global molecular changes in murine macrophage RAW 264.7 cells upon activation with CpG DNA and lipopolysaccharide (LPS) using Attenuated Total Reflectance (ATR) Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy. While CpG DNA and LPS are known to trigger innate immune responses, their impact on the relative amounts of nucleic acids, lipids, and proteins has not been previously characterized using bulk spectroscopic methods. The researchers employed ATR-FTIR to monitor cellular dynamics at 10 minutes, 30 minutes, 2 hours, and 6 hours post-treatment. Principal Component Analysis (PCA) revealed that PC-1 accounted for 83% of the variability, guiding the selection of specific spectral peaks for detailed analysis. The results indicate that both CpG DNA and LPS treatments lead to an early increase in total nucleic acid concentration. Specifically, RNA synthesis, identified by the ribose vibration peak at 915 cm⁻¹, occurs rapidly in the first two hours, followed by DNA synthesis starting around 6 hours post-CpG stimulation. In contrast, LPS treatment primarily induced RNA synthesis without significant DNA changes, consistent with its anti-proliferative nature. Protein analysis, using Amide I and II bands, showed a rapid initial increase in total protein concentration that leveled off after two hours. Furthermore, both stimuli triggered a significant increase in fatty acids, triglycerides, and cholesterol, suggesting a shared pathway of de novo lipogenesis during macrophage activation. This study demonstrates that FTIR spectroscopy serves as a rapid, non-destructive, and independent modality to monitor the activation dynamics of macrophages, providing a holistic view of metabolic shifts that complement traditional omics techniques.",
